#!/usr/bin/env python3
"""barry_launcher_shelld: Barry Launcher's apps and on-screen keyboard for
the AYN Thor's bottom screen.

Runs in barry_launcher_session next to the shell (shell/Shell.qml) and the
keyboard (shell/Keyboard.qml), which call it on 127.0.0.1:PORT:

  GET  /apps      {"browser": {"running": bool, "status": str}, ...}: status
                  is what a tile says while an app installs ("" otherwise)
  POST /launch    {"app": name}: start the app, or bring the running one
                  forward
  POST /close     {"app": name}: quit it
  POST /home      bring the home screen forward
  POST /apps/install {"path": archive}: install or update a user app
                  (barry_apps), {"ok": bool, "app": manifest | "error": str}
  POST /apps/remove  {"app": id}: remove a user app and its data
  GET  /game-links   {"links": [{"game", "gameName", "app", "appName", "close"}]}
  POST /game-links   {"links": [...]}: the apps that open with games
  POST /game-event   {"game": appid, "running": bool}: a game started or
                  stopped (the Barry Launcher plugin, which sees Steam)
  GET  /keyboard  {"visible": bool, "top": bool}: top when the text field is
                  in the lower half, so the keyboard does not cover it
  POST /keyboard  {"visible": bool}: show or hide by hand
  GET  /top-keyboard {"serial": n, "visible": bool}: the Trackpad app's
                  keyboard, asked up (or down) each time serial counts on
  POST /type      {"text": "..."} or {"key": "BackSpace"|"Return"|...}

A POST needs a JSON body, a Host of 127.0.0.1 or localhost and no Origin:
the apps here include a web browser, and a web page must not type.

The keyboard follows text focus through AT-SPI: Firefox reports focus
changes on the accessibility bus, and an editable field gaining focus shows
the keyboard; anything else gaining focus hides it. It only shows for a
touch, though: focus (or the caret moving in a field that already had it,
as when Signal's message box is tapped again) must come within TOUCH_S of a
finger on the bottom touchscreen, which this reads alongside gamescope. A
tap on the focused field itself also brings back a keyboard hidden by hand:
there the caret may not move at all.
Apps focusing their own fields as they open or come back stay keyboard-
free. Keys go to the focused
window through XTest (xdotool). The keyboard window is a gamescope overlay
(STEAM_OVERLAY), and while shown it has STEAM_INPUT_FOCUS=2, as Steam's own
keyboard does: it takes the touches, but X keyboard focus stays on the app
underneath. Like Steam's overlay it stays mapped and hides by opacity:
gamescope stops watching a window's properties once it is unmapped, so a
hidden overlay's marks could never be cleared. It also hides while the AYN
dashboard is up, since overlays are drawn above it.

Most apps are Firefox instances, each with its own profile under
~/.local/share/barry_launcher: the browser, and Discord's web app in kiosk
mode (no address bar or tabs). Signal has no web app: it is
Signal Desktop from Flathub, installed for the user on its first start.
Trackpad drives the top screen instead (shell/TopInputApp.qml, through
barry_launcher_inputd), in a window of its own like the rest: a trackpad
with a keyboard below it that slides away and back. A click on a top-screen
text field (focus over AT-SPI, within TOP_CLICK_S of a trackpad click or a
finger on the top screen) brings its keyboard up; so does a launch of
"keyboard" (its Keyboard tile, and the Barry Launcher plugin in place of
Steam's keyboard).
Without Steam, gamescope treats every window
as its own app and shows the most recently mapped one (stacking order does
not count), so switching is unmapping and mapping the wanted window again.
Holding the AYN button (sm8550-thor-backlightd) writes HOME_REQUEST, and any
change to it brings the home screen forward.

User apps (barry_apps: QML apps installed from an archive, by the barry-app
command or the Barry Launcher plugin) join the home screen after the built-in
ones; each runs in shell/AppHost.qml, with its service if it has one
(started first, stopped when the app ends), their output in
~/.cache/barry_launcher/ID.log. They are picked up as they are installed.
Those that come with Barry Launcher (Dino) are installed at its start
unless the user removed them.
"""
